If I were a junior WordPress developer in November 2026, I'd make a different reading list again.

Compared with Wix, WordPress requires a broader understanding of how websites work behind the scenes, particularly if you want to move beyond page builders and become a professional WordPress developer.

I'd want to become comfortable with:

- WordPress development and customization.
- Elementor, Gutenberg and the block editor.
- HTML, CSS, JavaScript and PHP.
- Custom themes and plugins.
- UI/UX and responsive design.
- WordPress CMS, custom post types and dynamic content.
- Website security, performance and SEO.

One important distinction: I'd learn both the visual website-building side of WordPress and its underlying development architecture.

WordPress is continuing to invest in block themes, Site Editing and collaboration. I'd therefore prioritize Gutenberg and modern block development rather than learning only traditional PHP themes.

