# 1. The books I'd read

## A. WordPress and web development foundations

Learning Web Design: A Beginner's Guide To Html, Css, Javascript, And Web Images Book By Jennifer Robbins,aaron Gustafson, ('tp') | Indigo

1. Learning Web Design — Jennifer Niederst Robbins

Essential foundation- HTML structure and semantics.
- CSS layouts and responsive design.
- JavaScript fundamentals.
- How browsers render websites.

Why: WordPress doesn't replace the need to understand web development. This gives you the foundation to customize almost anything.

Eloquent JavaScript, 4th Edition by Marijn Haverbeke - Penguin Books Australia

2. Eloquent JavaScript — Marijn Haverbeke

- JavaScript fundamentals.
- Functions, objects and arrays.
- Asynchronous programming.
- Working with APIs.

Why: Useful for custom WordPress functionality, interactive websites, Gutenberg blocks and modern frontend development.

PHP & MySQL: Server-side Web Development 1st Edition India | Ubuy

3. PHP & MySQL: Server-side Web Development — Jon Duckett

- Learn PHP syntax.
- Understand server-side programming.
- Work with databases.
- Understand how dynamic websites operate.

Why: PHP is fundamental to traditional WordPress themes, plugins and much of WordPress core. I'd make this a priority rather than treating PHP as optional.

CSS in Depth, Second Edition - Keith J. Grant

4. CSS in Depth — Keith J. Grant

- Flexbox and Grid.
- Responsive layouts.
- CSS architecture.
- Modern styling techniques.

Why: You'll frequently need to customize themes, page builders and blocks beyond their default styling.

## B. WordPress design and UI/UX

These would be especially important if I were building websites for clients.

Learnings from Refactoring UI. If you are a designer and do anything… | by Jatin Gupta | Medium

5. Refactoring UI

Why: Learn how to make WordPress websites look professionally designed instead of looking like generic theme installations.

Focus on typography, spacing, colors, buttons, cards and visual hierarchy.

Don't make me think - Libreria Santos Lectores

6. Don't Make Me Think

Why: Learn to create websites that are easy to navigate and understand.

Particularly useful when building business websites, ecommerce stores and landing pages.

Mastering the Art of User-Centric Design: Insights from ‘The Design of Everyday Things’ by Don Norman | by Saish Joshi | Medium

7. The Design of Everyday Things

Why: Understand human-centered design, usability, feedback and intuitive interactions.

It helps you think beyond how a website looks and consider how people actually use it.

## C. Professional WordPress development

Professional WordPress: Design and Development : Williams, Brad, Damstra, David, Stern, Hal: Amazon.in: Books

8. Professional WordPress: Design and Development

- Understand WordPress architecture.
- Learn theme and plugin concepts.
- Explore hooks, filters and customization.
- Understand how WordPress handles content.

Why: It's a useful WordPress-specific reference. However, I'd check the edition and treat older examples as historical where APIs have changed.

Pragmatic Programmer, The, 2nd Edition by David Thomas, Hardcover, 9780135957059 | Buy online at The Nile

9. The Pragmatic Programmer

- Debugging.
- Problem-solving.
- Automation.
- Writing maintainable code.

Why: It teaches you to think like a software developer rather than someone who simply installs plugins and themes.

Clean Code (2nd ed.) by Robert C. Martin (ebook)

10. Clean Code

- Code organization.
- Meaningful naming.
- Readability.
- Maintainability.

Why: Important when developing custom plugins, themes and functionality that other developers may need to maintain.

## D. SEO, accessibility and website optimization

The Art of SEO 3e - Eric Enge, Stephan Spencer, Jessie C. Stricchiola

11. The Art of SEO

- Technical SEO.
- Keyword research.
- Search intent.
- Website structure and indexing.

Why: WordPress clients often expect their developers to understand SEO, not just website construction.

Inclusive Design Patterns - Coding Accessibility Into Web Design by Heydon Pickering | Goodreads

12. Inclusive Design Patterns

- Accessible navigation.
- Semantic HTML.
- Accessible forms.
- Inclusive interface design.

Why: Accessibility is an important part of professional WordPress theme and plugin development.

# 2. What I'd learn specifically for WordPress in 2026

This is where I'd spend a lot of my practical learning time.

WordPress has two major development directions worth understanding:

A Beginner's Guide to the WordPress Block Editor (with Video) - LearnWoo

Modern WordPress: Gutenberg and block development

- Block Editor.
- Full Site Editing.
- Block themes.
- `theme.json`.
- Custom Gutenberg blocks.
- React and the WordPress JavaScript packages.

Genesis Framework vs Elementor: Which Is Better in 2025?

WordPress with page builders

- Elementor.
- Elementor Theme Builder.
- Dynamic content.
- Advanced Custom Fields (ACF).
- Custom post types.
- Custom CSS and JavaScript.

My advice: learn Gutenberg and Elementor, but don't become dependent on either.

If you understand WordPress itself, you can move between different themes, builders and development approaches.

