# 4. My six-month WordPress developer roadmap

If I were starting as a junior WordPress developer, this is how I'd organize my learning.

# 6-month development roadmap

1. Month 1 – HTML, CSS and design
   Read:
   - Learning Web Design
   - Refactoring UI
   Practice:
   - Build responsive layouts.
   - Recreate website designs.
   - Learn typography and spacing.
   Deliverable: A professional business website.
2. Month 2 – WordPress fundamentals
   Read:
   - Professional WordPress (as a reference)
   - Don't Make Me Think
   Practice:
   - Install WordPress locally.
   - Understand themes and plugins.
   - Learn the dashboard, pages, posts and menus.
   - Explore Gutenberg and Elementor.
   Deliverable: A complete business website.
3. Month 3 – PHP and JavaScript
   Read:
   - PHP & MySQL
   - Eloquent JavaScript
   Practice:
   - Write custom functions.
   - Understand hooks and filters.
   - Work with PHP templates.
   - Build interactive functionality.
   Deliverable: A custom WordPress feature.
4. Month 4 – CMS and dynamic development
   Practice:
   - Custom post types.
   - ACF and custom fields.
   - Dynamic templates.
   - WordPress REST API.
   - Custom queries.
   Deliverable: A real estate or directory website.
5. Month 5 – Advanced WordPress
   Practice:
   - Custom block themes.
   - `theme.json`.
   - Gutenberg block development.
   - Elementor Theme Builder.
   - Plugin development.
   Deliverable: A custom theme or plugin.
6. Month 6 – Professional standards
   Read:
   - The Pragmatic Programmer
   - Inclusive Design Patterns
   Practice:
   - Website security.
   - Performance optimization.
   - SEO.
   - Accessibility testing.
   - Git and GitHub.
   Deliverable: A polished, production-style portfolio project.

